    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In an elective monarchy the crown passes by election rather than inheritance; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> were usually few and powerful, and candidates often came from a single royal house.</li><li>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, whose rights the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> fixed in law, and the free elections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> are its best-known European forms.</li><li>Elections let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> bind each new ruler with conditions, but invited bribery, foreign meddling and disputed choices, and most elective crowns became hereditary or disappeared.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word conclave, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for “with a key”, recalls how cardinals were locked in until they chose a pope, a rule imposed in 1274 after an election at Viterbo had dragged on for almost three years.</p></aside>
    </section>

