    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">633</span><span class="rf-text">The Fourth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> rules that Visigothic kings be chosen by the nobles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">911</span><span class="rf-text">East Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> elect Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> king after the last Carolingian ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, dies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1356</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> fixes the seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and election by majority</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1438</span><span class="rf-text">Albert II’s election begins three centuries in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> choose only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, until 1740</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1573</span><span class="rf-text">The first free election of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> makes Henry of Valois king</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1619</span><span class="rf-text">The Bohemian estates depose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Ferdinand and elect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V of the Palatinate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1806</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> is dissolved, and its college of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> ends with it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-rulers">Conference of Rulers</a> elects the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a> for a five-year term</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-elector.jpg" alt="Prince-elector" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Prince-elector</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balduineum_Wahl_Heinrich_VII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In an elective monarchy the crown passes by election rather than by birth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> vary from case to case: the warriors of a people, the great nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of a kingdom, a fixed college of princes, the whole nobility gathered in one place, a council of hereditary rulers or, for the pope, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a>. So do the candidates. Most elective monarchies limited the choice to a royal kin group. Early Scandinavian kings were chosen from among the descendants of earlier kings; under tanistry, the Gaelic system of Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, the successor was chosen from among the male kin of the ruling family; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>’s constitution requires its king to be at least 30 years old and a descendant of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ang-duong">Ang Duong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-of-cambodia">King Norodom</a> or King Sisowath. Monarchs were usually elected for life, but modern federations have introduced fixed terms: the king of Malaysia serves for five years.</p><p>An election opens an interregnum, which every elective system had to manage. In early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, by tradition, the Senate named an interrex to oversee the choice of a new king; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> the Catholic primate served as interrex; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> entrusted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> during a vacancy to two imperial vicars, the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>. Many monarchs avoided an interregnum altogether by having a son elected in their own lifetime, as the early Capetian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Staufen emperors did.</p><p>Election also gave the electors a hold over the elected. From the 16th century each King of the Romans swore to an electoral capitulation, a contract granting rights to the electors and the princes of the Empire, and every king of Poland–Lithuania swore to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrician-articles">Henrician Articles</a> and to his own pacta conventa, which recognised the nobility’s right to resist a king who broke the law. Elective monarchy thus stands between hereditary monarchy and a republic. No one is born to the throne, though the repeated election of a ruler’s sons often made a crown hereditary in practice; yet unlike an elected president, the elected ruler holds a royal title, usually for life, with the sacred and dynastic dignity of kingship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson.jpg" alt="Harold Godwinson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_tapestry_stitches_detail..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">~, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The election of kings is older than the written record of most peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> observed in his Germania (98 CE) that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> chose their kings for their noble birth and their war leaders for their courage, and described assemblies in which free warriors approved proposals by clashing their spears. Roman tradition held that the kings of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> were chosen by the people’s assemblies after an interregnum, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, according to ancient writers, the assembled army acclaimed new kings. In Asia the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> were chosen from the Arsacid family by a council of nobles, and the Korean chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> records that the first king of Silla was chosen by an assembly of village elders in 57 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> raised their khans at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>, a gathering of princes and commanders, which proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> Genghis Khan in 1206.</p><p>In early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> election and inheritance were mixed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> was at least partly elective: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> was elected king of the Franks in 751 with papal approval, and the West Frankish nobles chose between rival lineages until they elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> in 987. The Fourth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> declared in 633 that Visigothic kings should be chosen by the nobles of the whole people together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the witan, the council of the realm’s leading men, chose among the members of the royal house and in 1066 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> king. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath">Declaration of Arbroath</a> of 1320 announced that the Scottish nobles would choose another king if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> ever submitted to England.</p><p>The East Frankish kingdom, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, made election its lasting principle. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, the last Carolingian ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, died in 911, the dukes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> elected Conrad, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, as their king, and in 919 chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. Under the Ottonian and Salian dynasties the princes usually did little more than confirm hereditary succession, but the right to elect survived and grew. Political writers weighed the two principles. Defenders of hereditary succession, including Jean Bodin in his Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576), argued that elections bred faction and civil war, while Polish nobles valued election because each candidate had to promise to respect their privileges, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> did in the first such undertaking in 1291.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski.jpg" alt="Stanisław August Poniatowski" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanis%C5%82aw_II_August_Poniatowski_in_coronation_clothes.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcello Bacciarelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> narrowed as the princes grew stronger. After the election of Lothair III in 1125, more open than those before it, a small group of princes had by the 13th century won the right to elect the King of the Romans, who was later crowned emperor by the pope. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> wrote in 1263 that by “immemorial custom” seven princes did so: the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>. Their split in 1257 had produced two kings, Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>. The Declaration of Rhense of 1338 held that the electors’ majority alone made a king, without papal confirmation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> fixed in law the seven electors, majority voting, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> as the place of election and the electors’ privileges. From 1438 to 1740 the electors always chose a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, and the college, enlarged in the 17th century, ended with the Empire in 1806.</p><p>Elsewhere the estates chose their kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was elective until 1687, when its diet accepted hereditary Habsburg succession. The Bohemian estates, which had elected Habsburgs since 1526, deposed the Habsburg Ferdinand in 1619 and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V of the Palatinate</a>, the “Winter King”; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620 the Habsburgs abolished the elective crown. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, when the Jagiellonian line ended with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> in 1572, the nobles decided that every nobleman who came to the election field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> should vote. Henry of Valois, elected in 1573, was the first king so chosen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a>, elected in 1764, the last; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a> made the throne hereditary, but Poland–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was partitioned out of existence by 1795.</p><p>Beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, councils of princes and officials in Siam chose kings when the succession was unclear, as in 1824 and 1868. The popes, monarchs of the Papal States until 1870 and of Vatican City since 1929, were elected by the cardinals from 1059, by a two-thirds majority from 1179 and, under a rule first imposed in 1274, behind locked doors. New states also turned to elected kings: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>’s parliament chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Prince Carl of Denmark</a> as Haakon VII in 1905, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>’s elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-frederick-charles-of-hesse">Prince Frederick Charles of Hesse</a> in 1918, though he never reigned, and at independence in 1957 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-rulers">Conference of Rulers</a> began to elect a federal king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1356" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldene_Bulle_Nahaufnahme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karl IV., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defenders of election argued that it put the ablest candidate on the throne. Medieval councils set aside child heirs in favour of grown men: John was chosen King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1199 because his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany">Arthur of Brittany</a>, the heir by strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, was a boy, and Parliament chose Henry IV in 1399 over the young Earl of March. Polish nobles preferred to keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a> but to choose among its sons, believing that princes who had to earn the crown would be raised to be more capable. In 1787 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> told the Constitutional Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> that the American executive should be an elected monarch serving during “good behaviour”, that is, for life unless impeached, strong enough to resist foreign corruption and yet controlled enough to prevent tyranny at home.</p><p>Election was also a way of limiting royal power and sharing it among the great. The electoral capitulations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the pacta conventa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> were contracts between ruler and ruled, and historians credit the free elections with preventing the rise of an absolute monarchy in the Commonwealth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-confederation">Warsaw Confederation</a> of 1573, agreed during the first interregnum, guaranteed religious peace among the nobility, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrician-articles">Henrician Articles</a> bound each new king to keep it, so that the Commonwealth remained a land of relative religious toleration in an age of religious wars. In the Holy Roman Empire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> gave the electoral system rules that lasted 450 years and bound a federation of hundreds of principalities, church lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> to a common head chosen by consent.</p><p>The form has also proved adaptable to federal states. In Malaysia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-rulers">Conference of Rulers</a> has elected the king by secret ballot since 1957, and the office has in practice rotated among the nine hereditary rulers of the Malay states, giving each state a turn and binding all of them to the federation. The United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, formed in 1971, likewise elects its president from among the rulers of its seven emirates. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> shows how long an elective monarchy can last: election by the cardinals has endured since the 11th century, giving the Catholic Church each time a leader chosen from among its senior clergy rather than the heir of a dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia.jpg" alt="Philip of Swabia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vad-0321_040_Philipp_von_Schwaben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The gravest weakness of elective monarchy was the contest for the crown. Each vacancy invited rival candidates, and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> produced civil wars. The double election of 1198 set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> against Otto IV in a long war for the German crown; the double election of 1257 left the Empire with two absentee kings during what historians call the Great Interregnum; and after the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Great Khan Möngke</a> in 1259 rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultais</a> elected his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>, whose war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluid-civil-war">Toluid Civil War</a>, hastened the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> saw double elections in 1575, 1587, 1697 and 1733, the last setting off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>’s election of Frederick V in 1619 helped to turn a local revolt into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a>.</p><p>Elections invited bribery and foreign interference. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, won the imperial election of 1519 with money lent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger family</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II the Strong</a>, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, became a Catholic in 1697 in order to stand for the Polish throne. In Poland–Lithuania foreign dynasties competed for the crown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> could steer the votes of the poorer nobility, and electors were tempted to choose a weak and controllable king. In 1764 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> was elected with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> and with Russian troops in the country. By the Commonwealth’s last years royal elections had come to be seen as a source of conflict and a symbol of anarchy, and many historians have counted them among the causes of the weakness that ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland">Partitions of Poland</a> between 1772 and 1795.</p><p>The conditions that electors imposed could also hollow out royal government. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> the electors’ privileges, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a>’s ban on appeals from their subjects to the imperial courts, strengthened the princes at the expense of the emperor. In Poland–Lithuania the free election belonged to a system of noble liberties that also included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>, used from 1652 to break up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> by a single deputy’s objection. Elective crowns also tended to become hereditary in practice, as when the Capetians had their sons elected in their own lifetimes or the electors kept choosing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>; where they did not, every succession reopened the contest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals.jpg" alt="College of Cardinals" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinals_in_red_vestments_during_the_funeral_mass_for_Pope_Francis_in_2025_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The White House, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The elective principle left its mark on European constitutional thought. Electoral capitulations and pacta conventa were early written limits on a ruler’s power, and the idea that a ruler’s authority comes from the choice of the community rather than from birth alone passed into theories of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and into the elected presidencies of modern republics. The debates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1787 show the connection: Hamilton’s proposal of an elected monarch was rejected in favour of a president serving a four-year term, with the possibility of re-election.</p><p>Several states still choose their monarchs by election. The pope, sovereign of Vatican City, is elected for life by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a> in a conclave and needs a two-thirds majority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a> of Malaysia is elected for five years by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-rulers">Conference of Rulers</a>, and the state of Negeri Sembilan chooses its own ruler through a council of ruling chiefs. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> the nine-member Royal Council of the Throne, which includes the prime minister, the presiding officers of both houses of parliament and the two Buddhist patriarchs, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihamoni">Norodom Sihamoni</a> as king in 2004. The president of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> is elected by the Federal Supreme Council, though the office has in practice always gone to the ruler of Abu Dhabi. Andorra’s co-princes are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Urgell, appointed by the pope, and the President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, elected by French voters, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> the Kīngitanga, the Māori King Movement founded in 1858, chooses its monarch through an assembly of chiefs.</p><p>Historians now judge the great elective monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> more kindly than many of their 19th-century predecessors did. Nationalist historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>’s elective constitution as a cause of German weakness and disunity, and Polish historians long debated whether the free elections and the nobles’ liberties had doomed the Commonwealth. Recent work, such as Joachim Whaley’s Germany and the Holy Roman Empire (2012) and Peter H. Wilson’s Heart of Europe (2016), presents the Empire as a working system that kept peace and law among its many members for centuries, while historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> such as Norman Davies have emphasised its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> and its tradition of limited, consensual government, without denying the dangers its elections created.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Elective monarchy made the crown a prize to be conferred rather than inherited. From the East Frankish election of 911 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> to the free elections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, it let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> choose capable rulers and bind them by law, but often brought disputed successions, bribery and foreign meddling; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and Malaysia keep it today.</em></p>

